WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:08.280
All right, moving on to another common use case for the CTAs that I usually use as well.

00:08.280 --> 00:14.960
In my projects, we use CTAs in order to create a persisted snapshot of the data at a specific time

00:14.960 --> 00:17.560
in order to analyze data quality issue.

00:17.600 --> 00:19.120
So let's understand what this means.

00:19.160 --> 00:22.840
Now in some scenarios you have like a table and you are analyzing an issue.

00:22.840 --> 00:25.040
So there is like a data quality issue at your data.

00:25.040 --> 00:28.880
And you are analyzing this scenario in order to understand why it happens.

00:28.880 --> 00:34.960
But the problem is that at the same time, there will be updates on the table and your data is changing.

00:34.960 --> 00:40.240
So there will be updates, maybe on some fields, or you are getting new records and everything is getting

00:40.240 --> 00:46.200
mixed up, and you will not be able to analyze the scenario where the data quality issue happened.

00:46.200 --> 00:50.640
So now it's almost impossible to find the root cause of your issue.

00:50.640 --> 00:56.400
But instead of that, what we do if we have like an issue of the data, we go and create a fixed, persisted

00:56.400 --> 01:03.320
snapshot of the data in a separate table using CTAs so that we make sure nothing is changing and everything

01:03.320 --> 01:03.920
is fixed.

01:03.920 --> 01:09.760
And with that, I can keep doing my analysis on the same data without the worry that data are getting

01:09.760 --> 01:10.360
changed.

01:10.360 --> 01:15.560
So this is another way why we use CTAs in our projects to make sure that we have a snapshot of the data

01:15.560 --> 01:22.440
to ensure that our analyses are done on the same scenario that caused the bug, and can be used as a

01:22.440 --> 01:25.960
foundation for finding the problem and fixing it.
